    every region contains 2-5 dots; min Manhattan distance between dots >=2
  transform: recolor each isolated BLUE dot to the unique majority marker color bordering its region; walls and markers unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: construct wall graph first, then place markers ensuring majority constraint, then scatter dots
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size in range. Design a rectilinear planar graph on the grid -- place 8-17 node positions at intersections/edges, connect adjacent nodes with H/V blue wall segments of length 2-11.  Ensure walls+markers form one connected component and partition grid into 6-11 regions.
    2. Choose 3-4 marker colors. Assign a color to each node such that every region has a unique strict majority among its bordering nodes. Greedy or constraint propagation works since regions share nodes.
    3. For each region, scatter 2-5 isolated BLUE dots on BLACK cells with no BLUE 4-neighbor (min Manhattan distance 2 between dots).
    4. Output is derived by running the solver (flood-fill regions, count marker votes, recolor dots).
